# TypeScript style

- Keep strict mode enabled.
- Avoid `any`; use `unknown` plus explicit narrowing.
- Use `PascalCase` for classes, components, and types; `camelCase` for functions, methods, and variables; `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` only for true global constants.
- Use semantic boolean prefixes such as `is`, `has`, `can`, or `should`.
- Use `kebab-case` filenames except for Next.js special files.
- Name important limits, percentages, statuses, and conversion factors instead of using magic values.
- Prefer small functions, early returns, immutable data, and one main responsibility per file.
- Use type-only imports where appropriate and keep imports grouped consistently.
- Do not add `eslint-disable`, `@ts-ignore`, unsafe assertions, or casts merely to silence diagnostics.
- Do not require `I` prefixes or ceremonial base classes.

Document exported domain/public contracts when their semantics are not evident. Do not add `@ai` tags or redundant JSDoc.
